# English narration for both videos. Same tuple layout as the Chinese vo.py: (opening, purpose sentence, body, purpose-card short text).
PLATFORM = {
 "s1": ("", "", "Hi, I'm Little Dragon! Today I'll show you the Dragon Platform, and how a whole month of data turns, step by step, into one monthly report.", ""),
 "bg": ("First, the background.", "", "Every month, the operations team has to produce a service quality report. The data is scattered across several systems. You log in to each one and export it, tidy everything into tables, work out the pass rates by hand, chase people one by one to fix issues, and finally lay it all out as a report. It takes days, and mistakes creep in. The Dragon Platform links this whole routine into one automatic pipeline.", ""),
 "ov": ("", "", "The whole pipeline has five stations: Data Capture, Data Import, Report Board, One-click Nudge, and Monthly Report. Scripts capture data, rules calculate metrics, the board scores and saves, nudges chase weak spots, and the monthly report wraps up.", ""),
 "s2": ("Station one: Data Capture.", "Your data is scattered across systems. Exporting by hand is slow and easy to get wrong, so a script pulls it all back in one click.", "This is the Script Workshop. First install the UI.Vision extension; Chrome and Edge both work. On the left is the smart script library. The default scripts work out of the box, and you can sort, rename, import and export them.", "Manual export is slow and error-prone, scripts do it in one click"),
 "s3": ("", "No ready-made script? No coding needed. Three steps turn an API request into a capture script.", "Pick a request address, for example DataFab, or one of the NetCare regional nodes. Paste the request payload. Then paste a sample JSON response. You can copy both the payload and the response from the Network panel of your browser's developer tools.", "No code, three steps to a capture script"),
 "s4": ("", "This step is about capturing the right data, and all of it.", "What if the response holds several tables? Type a keyword in Data Focus and the system scores each table and picks the best match. If several match, a pop-up lets you choose. Then switch on the options you need: Auto Paging to capture every page, Summary Fallback, and Dynamic CPC and NID. When a report spans two months, turn on Current plus Previous Month, so the start of the month never misses last month's data.", "Capture the right data, and all of it"),
 "s5": ("", "Save finished scripts to the library, then rerun them in one click every month.", "Click Generate to get a production-grade UI.Vision macro, or copy the console script instead. Happy with it? Add it to the sidebar. It is sorted automatically, and AI even suggests a name. Hit an unfamiliar website? Use the AI adapter: paste Copy as fetch and a sample response, sanitize it first, then analyze, and you do not have to study the API yourself.", "Save to the library, rerun every month in one click"),
 "s6": ("", "Captured data has to get into the platform. This step saves you the download, rename and upload chores.", "In the library, tick your scripts and click Run Batch. It pages through the data automatically. When it is done, the data goes straight to the Data Import page. One reminder: from capture until import finishes, please do not switch tenants.", "No more download, rename, upload"),
 "s7": ("Station two: Data Import.", "Import turns scattered tables into one data source the platform can calculate with.", "Click Batch Import All Tables. The system sorts the files by filename prefix into five types: Remediation, Routine Risk, Special Risk, SR Details and Vulnerability Alerts. Then it shows red, yellow and green warnings by status and due date, so you see at a glance which ticket is about to run late.", "Scattered tables become one data source"),
 "s8": ("", "Why set up metric rules? Set them once, and every month's import calculates the numbers for you, with no manual counting.", "Click the Metrics button on a table and pick a mode. Extract One Row: where column X contains Y, take the value in column Z from that row. Extract Many Rows: with the same condition, add up column Z, or take the average, maximum or minimum, or count the valid entries.", "Set a rule once, metrics appear every month"),
 "s9": ("", "", "Count Times: among rows where column X contains Y, count the rows where column Z contains keyword K. Count Ratio: the rows where Z contains K, divided by the rows where X contains Y. Leave X empty and it divides by all rows. Return rate and overdue rate in your assessments are calculated exactly this way, and conditions can test for empty or filled cells.", ""),
 "s10": ("", "", "Conditions not enough? Turn on Advanced Filters and add more column conditions: AND means all must match, OR means any one will do. The data range can be the current table, or all independent tables. A rule can be a main metric on its own, or hang under a main metric as a child metric, so one metric can be split by office, and you can see at a glance who is dragging it down.", ""),
 "s11": ("", "With a target in place, the system can tell whether you hit it, and light up warnings by itself.", "In Alert Settings, set a target and a warning direction for each month, January to December. In All Rules you can search and copy rules, map templates quickly, and paste two columns from Excel to fill in dozens of rules in seconds. After any rule change, the system recalculates by itself, so you do not need to upload again.", "Targets let the system judge and warn"),
 "s12": ("Station three: Report Board.", "The board turns piles of metrics into scores and rankings, so you can see at a glance who is doing well and who is not.", "Choose the assessment month and the board scores each metric against its target, giving you a health ranking of customer groups and offices. You can add metrics by hand and set weights, groups and a bilingual dictionary. Others are only monitored, not scored. Click Save to store a snapshot, map it with a template, or export the race table.", "Piles of metrics become scores and rankings"),
 "s13": ("Station four: One-click Nudge.", "No more hunting through tables for weak spots, or writing reminder messages one by one.", "It reads the latest saved snapshot and automatically picks out the metrics that lost points or are overdue, and anything matching an ignore keyword is hidden. In the template settings, write the greeting, title and closing, and use MM and DD for the month and date.", "Finds weak spots, writes the nudge"),
 "s14": ("", "", "Send them to specific people? In Advanced Distribution, first add people and groups to the roster, then click a category card to assign. Network name mapping matches tickets to categories. It supports direct chat, group chat, email CC and meeting invites. You can also copy the WeLink script, or download an Excel weak spot matrix. Everyone only receives the tickets that concern them, so nudges hit the mark.", ""),
 "s15": ("Station five: Monthly Report page.", "Numbers in the report come straight from the data library and always match the source tables, with no copy and paste.", "It is a three column workspace: the library on the left, the canvas in the middle, settings on the right. Create a report and choose the report month. In the library you can browse raw tables, metrics and topics for any past month without touching the current report. Drag a worksheet onto the canvas and it becomes a table.", "Numbers link to the library and match the source"),
 "s16": ("", "", "The canvas offers five component types: Text, Metric, Table, Pivot Chart and Rule Summary. Charts work like Excel: drag fields into Filters, Columns, Rows and Values, and switch between seven chart types any time. Type the at sign in the text to insert a metric number, and two neighboring metrics can even form a formula. Change the month and everything recalculates, so numbers are never copied by hand, or copied wrong.", ""),
 "s17": ("", "Next month you do not redo the report. Change the month and the whole thing updates.", "Behind this are month relations: This Month, Last Month, Same Month Last Year, an offset month, or a fixed month. Bind both this month and last month to one chart, and you get month over month. Switch to a new month and a comparison preview pops up showing Updated, Consistent, Fixed Version and Pending. Tick the ones to apply, and you can undo it.", "Next month, change the month and update it all"),
 "s18": ("", "", "Finally, save to the server, check the layout in the preview, and export as HTML, PDF, a long image, or an Outlook email. Capture, import, board, nudge, report: five stations done. Go and give it a try!", ""),
}

NETCARE = {
 "s1": "Hi, I'm Audie! Today I'll walk you through the NetCare RFC Plans and AI Audit tool. First, the problem it solves. Then four steps: get the installer, add it to your browser, start using it, and tweak the settings, with every click shown.",
 "bg": "First, the background. An RFC is a network change request, and every request comes with a change plan document. Auditors have to open the requests one by one, download each plan, and check it line by line against a checklist. With lots of requests, it is slow and things get missed. This little tool lives in your browser. It downloads plans in bulk, lets AI do a first pass, and you only read the verdicts.",
 "s2": "Step one: get the installer. Open the Dragon Platform home page, type Extension Packager in the search box, and open F12 Extension Packager. On the left choose Built-in Scripts, click NetCare RFC Plans and AI Audit, then click Generate. You will download a zip file, and that zip is the installer for this tool.",
 "s3": "Step two: add it to your browser. First unzip the file to get a folder. Then type chrome://extensions in the address bar. In Edge, type edge://extensions. Press Enter to open the extensions page. Turn on Developer mode at the top right, then click Load unpacked at the top left, choose the folder you just unzipped, and you are done!",
 "s4": "Step three: start using it. Open the NetCare website, log in, and refresh the page. Then click the puzzle icon at the top right of your browser, find this tool in the list, and click Start Script. A small window pops up at the bottom right of the page.",
 "s4b": "In the small window, paste one or more RFC numbers, the numbers of the change requests you want to check, up to fifty at a time. Click Find and Download, and you will see how far each request has got, one line at a time.",
 "s5": "While downloading the Word plan, it can also open the plan online and automatically find its chapters and attachments. Tick the chapters you care about and pack them into a zip in one click. A screenshot of each chapter exactly as it looks on the page is saved too, as evidence for later.",
 "s6": "With AI Audit switched on, AI follows the rules you wrote and checks every chapter line by line. There are only three verdicts: Pass, Fail, or Needs manual review. You can also export everything as an Excel report in one click.",
 "s7": "Last, the settings. Click the gear icon at the top right of the small window. The General tab lets you choose how many requests download at once and how many seconds to wait between them, and switch on Auto Extract and AI Audit. On the Chapter Rules tab, tick the chapters to check, then write one line of instructions for each, just like briefing a colleague.",
 "s8": "The AI Connection tab tells the tool which AI to use. First pick a provider: OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, or even an in-house local service. Then fill in its web address, the model name and the key, which works like a password. Click Test Connection first, and once it works, click Save and Use for Audit. The key stays on your own computer, and sensitive details are masked before anything is sent to the AI.",
 "s9": "Finally, set a backup password of at least eight characters and export an encrypted backup. Even on a new computer, you can restore with one click. That is it! Installer, install, use, settings, you have learned it all. Go and give it a try!",
}

INCENTIVE = {
 "s1": ("", "", "Hi, I'm Little Dragon! Today I'll cover Operation Incentive Statistics: how it turns each month's night operations into everyone's incentive, and, more importantly, how it makes sure every single payout is accurate and checks out.", ""),
 "bg": ("First, the background.", "", "Every month, colleagues who did night operations earn an incentive. The data comes from an Excel sheet with thousands of rows. You have to pick out the night operations that were truly completed. If one person did several tickets on the same day, only the highest level counts, once. Then you apply the amount for each level, and finally total it up per person. Doing this by hand is slow, it is easy to miss things or make mistakes, and it affects what each person is paid. So this tool does three things: it calculates automatically, every step can be checked, and everyone confirms the result.", ""),
 "ov": ("", "", "The process has four steps: import the data, set the rules, verify accuracy, and export for confirmation. Verifying accuracy is the most important step.", ""),
 "s2": ("Step one: import the data.", "Drop in this month's night operations sheet as it is, with no manual tidying first.", "Click Choose Excel file and pick the sheet exported for the month. Once imported, the top of the page shows the file name and the number of records. The system also splits people into non WX and WX groups, depending on whether the operator ID contains WX, and counts them separately so they never get mixed up.", "Import directly, no manual tidying"),
 "s3": ("Step two: set the rules.", "The amounts and the night hours are rules you can see and change. It is not a black box.", "Click Rule Settings. Here you set the incentive amount for each of four levels: Low, Medium, High and Fatal. The defaults are fifty, one eighty, two eighty and six hundred. Tick Night operations only, and the hours default to ten at night until six in the morning. You can also choose which column identifies the operator. After editing, click Save and Apply, and the results recalculate automatically.", "Rules you can see and change"),
 "s4": ("", "Every record has to pass four gates before it counts toward the incentive.", "First, the task must be completed. Second, if the night rule is on, the operation time must fall in the night window. Third, there must be an operator and a start time. Pass these three gates and it becomes a valid record by the rules. Fourth, for the same person on the same day, even with several tickets, only the highest level is counted, once. For example, if one day has a Low, a High and a Fatal ticket, only the Fatal six hundred counts.", "Four gates before it counts"),
 "s5": ("Step three: verify accuracy.", "Records that are not counted never quietly disappear. Every one can be traced.", "Switch to Exceptions, and every record that did not pass states its reason, for example task not completed, not a night operation, or missing operator. Then open Calculation Details. Every row is marked Counted or Ignored, and ignored rows say why, for example several tickets on the same day, already counted at a higher level. For any payout, you can find out why it was counted, and why it was not.", "Every record can be traced"),
 "s6": ("", "", "Below the table, a Data Quality Check panel looks for problems for you. Name and ID mismatch: the operator ID does not match the ID attached to the name in the sheet. Unrecognized level: these records are treated as zero, and the level needs filling in. Night field and time disagree: the two ways of judging give different answers. Every item can be opened to see the details. Note that these are only warnings. They never change the results behind your back. You make the call.", ""),
 "s7": ("", "Month boundaries are where mistakes happen most easily.", "For example, an operation that runs from late at night on the last day of the month into the early hours of the first. By local start time it belongs to last month, but by the year and month columns in the original sheet it may belong to next month. So choose the basis for assigning months under Period Basis. The page also warns you: confirm the basis before exporting, so nothing is missed and incentive amounts are not affected.", "Month boundaries trip people up"),
 "s8": ("", "Incentives go only to people who are on the job, so check each ID's current status.", "Click Download WeLink Person Query script. It covers every ID in the sheet. Run it, and it looks up the IDs one by one and produces a text result. Then click Import WeLink results. The system tags people. If the lookup comes back empty, they are tagged Possibly resigned. If an ID has changed, they are tagged ID changed, and their department can be shown too. Possibly resigned is only a hint, and you must confirm it by hand a second time.", "Check that each person is still on the job"),
 "s9": ("", "", "What if you find a problem? There are three ways to handle it. First, the Incentive Blacklist: enter an ID, optionally with a reason. People on the list stay in the details, but are no longer paid. Second, Exclusion Rules: set a condition on any column, or on a WeLink person field, and everyone who matches is not paid. Third, the Meeting Attendance Alert: if someone who earned an incentive was absent or late in the meeting attendance snapshots, the page warns you. Tick them and add them to the blacklist in one click, or export a checklist. After handling, the results are recalculated automatically.", ""),
 "s10": ("", "", "Once checked, you can look at the results. At the top you can filter quickly by this month, last month, this quarter and so on, or filter by BU, customer, person and level. There are six views: Dimension, Period, Period Cross, Person, Details and Exceptions. There are also year over year and month over month comparisons, and a row of charts where you click a shape to drill down to the person or month. The number cards always show the raw records, valid records, deduplicated records, headcount and incentive total for the current scope, so the numbers tie out from start to finish.", ""),
 "s11": ("Step four: export and confirm.", "Calculating is not the end. Everyone and their supervisors must confirm, and only then does it count.", "Click Export Current Results. The exported file keeps every column of the original sheet, keeps only the rows that were paid, and adds the row incentive amount, the WeLink department, and the ID change. So every incentive can be checked against the original record. Send it to each supervisor and colleague to confirm, and for anyone marked Possibly resigned, the supervisor must confirm in person. If anything is in doubt, go back to the tool, add to the blacklist or change the rules, then export again.", "Calculated, then confirmed by everyone"),
 "s12": ("", "", "Once confirmed, save a snapshot. Click Save Snapshot, give it a name and a period, for example September 2026. The system stores the original data, the rules and the blacklist on the server together. Later, in the Snapshot Library, you can reload that exact workspace in one click, and look back to see how that month was calculated.", ""),
 "s13": ("", "", "To sum up: the rules are open and editable, every record has a destination, anomalies raise their own alerts, and finally a person confirms. The tool does the calculating, people do the confirming. Every incentive is clear, and it all checks out. Go and give it a try!", ""),
}

EOS = {
 "s1": ("", "", "Hi, I'm Little Dragon! Today's topic is EOS risk management and incorporation: how to pull the latest progress from the system in one click, how to forecast what we can reach this year, and how the monthly report is generated automatically.", ""),
 "bg": ("First, the background.", "", "EOS means a product or version has reached end of service. These old products and versions still run in customer networks, so when something fails, support is limited and the risk is high. That is why we track them every month: products must be retired from the network, versions must be upgraded, and together we call this incorporation. We track every customer, every product line, and every product. In the past, you logged in and collected numbers page by page, counted them by hand, asked each account team for this year's planned numbers, worked out yourself whether the annual target could be met, and finally wrote up a bilingual monthly report. It was slow and error-prone, and it was hard to see in time where there was no plan. This feature links the whole process together.", ""),
 "ov": ("", "", "The process has five steps. One, copy the floating panel script. Two, capture data on the NetCare site. Three, fill in plans and forecast the target. Four, export and import back into the platform. Five, generate the monthly report in one click. Repeat it every month to keep tracking.", ""),
 "s2": ("Step one: copy the floating panel script.", "NetCare data can only be read on the NetCare site, so the capture script has to run there.", "Open the Data Capture page and click the Floating Panel Script button in the library on the left. A window opens to choose a site, listing each site and how many scripts it can run. Because of the browser's same-origin rule, pick the site you will open and paste the script into. Here, choose NetCare China, which has a dozen or so scripts, and click Copy This Site. Note that the floating panel does not download CSV files one by one. After capturing, you view them in the panel, or download them all as one package.", "The script must run on the NetCare site"),
 "s3": ("Step two: capture data on the NetCare site.", "", "Log in to the NetCare site with your usual account, press F12 to open the developer tools, go to the Console, paste the script you just copied, and press Enter. A data capture panel pops up on the page. At this point the script is loaded, but capture has not started. Check that the site and your login are right, then click Start Capture. The panel takes over the data, so there are no files to download one by one.", ""),
 "s4": ("", "No more logging in page by page. One refresh, and the latest progress in the system is all in hand.", "At the top of the panel there is a NetCare Insights button. Click it to open the insights view. Click Refresh, and the panel pulls five kinds of data: certificates, EOS, change volume, high-risk interception, and SR, shown as five tabs. This time we focus on the first one, EOS Incorporation.", "One refresh, all the latest progress"),
 "s5": ("", "First the big picture: each customer's incorporation rate, and how far it is from the target.", "At the top of the EOS Incorporation page you can set a product target and a version target. The defaults are thirty and eighty percent, and you can adjust them as needed. The summary table below lists each customer: quantity, rate, pending, plan this year, no plan, and projected rate, one set for products and one for versions. A rate that meets the target is green, one that misses it is red, so you can see at a glance which customer is falling behind.", "The big picture: how far from target"),
 "s6": ("", "Update the planned number of network elements any time, and see right away what can be reached this year.", "Expand the rows to go down by customer, product line, and product; versions are grouped by product as well. Every item has a plan for this year. Click plus or minus, or type a number, and it cannot exceed the pending count. Next to it is a progress note to record where the follow-up stands. Every change instantly updates the projected rate of that item, that customer, and the whole table. The incorporation brief is recalculated too: how many more from the no-plan list would lift the projected rate, to what level, and how many percentage points remain to the target.", "Change a plan, see the yearly forecast"),
 "s7": ("", "", "The no-plan items are the ones that need a push. The rightmost column of the summary table lists, for each customer, the products or versions with the most no-plan units, that is the Top N, and you can set N to any number on the page. Sort by quantity from high to low, or by rate from low to high. With this list, it is clear at a glance which customer, product, or version still has no plan, so you can take it to the neighboring teams and push incorporation forward together. Tables can be collapsed or shown full screen, and exported straight to Excel.", ""),
 "s8": ("Step four: export, then import into the platform.", "The plans, targets, and notes you entered must come back to the platform before the report can be generated.", "Click Export JSON. The panel saves all the captured data, together with the targets, planned numbers, and notes you entered, into one file. Back on the platform's Data Capture page, open the Floating Panel Script window again, and this time click Simulate Panel next to NetCare China, then click Import JSON. It works offline and never calls the live site. After import it enters review mode and is archived to the server automatically. The page shows Archived, so you can look back month by month.", "Bring the data back to auto-write the report"),
 "s9": ("Step five: generate the monthly report in one click.", "Once imported, the monthly report is one click away.", "Open the Topic Analysis page. At the top is the snapshot overview: total snapshots, how many batches NetCare and DataFab have archived, and the latest data time. Below is the report area. The report topic defaults to EOS incorporation, and you can switch to certificates, changes, high-risk interception, SR, and other topics. Choose the report month and the report is generated immediately. Each month uses the last snapshot imported for that month, and the planned numbers and targets come from the settings saved in that snapshot, so later edits never change past reports.", "Imported, then one click to the report"),
 "s10": ("", "", "The report is clearly structured. It opens with the title and the annual overall targets: a one hundred percent risk closure rate, plus three levels, baseline, target, and stretch, for both the product retirement rate and the version incorporation rate. Next comes the progress overview, written automatically: the current status, the projected year-end completion rate, how many units still have no plan, which customers are involved, and recommendations. Then the progress details: one table each for products and versions, listing by customer the total, incorporated, current rate, pending, plan this year, no plan, and rate after plan, with key focus text above the table. Finally, risk management progress and the risk handling process.", ""),
 "s11": ("", "", "The report is not rigid. Every text and table can be edited with one click, and selected text can be made bold or recolored. If you mess up the generated text for the month, click Restore Monthly Text, and fixed sections can be restored with Restore Fixed Text. In Mapping and Threshold Settings, you can map customer names to the names you want to show, and set a display threshold for key equipment counts, so small numbers are left out. Chinese and English are two separate versions. When you edit the Chinese, the page flags English awaiting sync, and if the platform has an AI assistant configured, AI Translate does it in one click.", ""),
 "s12": ("", "", "When it looks right, export it. The long PNG comes in Chinese, English, and a combined bilingual version, and you can download all three in one click. You can also download a PDF, an HTML file you can browse offline, a multi-sheet Excel workbook, and an MSG email you can keep editing in Outlook. To keep a record, save a snapshot into the report snapshot library. Report Compare shows two versions side by side and highlights differences in text and data automatically. You can also export a project file and share it, and a colleague who imports it can keep editing.", ""),
 "s13": ("", "", "Finally, how to make it a habit. Every month, repeat the five steps: capture, plan, export and import, and generate the report, with no manual compiling. Targets can be refreshed every month to keep tracking. The value comes in four parts. One, the latest progress in the system, fast. Two, refresh planned numbers any time and forecast directly whether the yearly target can be met. Three, the Top N list keeps everyone focused on the products and versions not yet incorporated. Four, the report writes itself, saving a lot of compiling time. Go and give it a try!", ""),
}
